Z||000009||85ZainoGT||12-16-2001||02:42 PM||||ya, there is few things I can do to take my mind totally away, the one thing that does it all the time is basketball, I think I jus need tobe happier with myself and with what I have...<P> Triad -- lol..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1987 GT all the options :)<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpicNEW3bsmall2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||12-16-2001||04:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>not trying to steal the spotlight here or anything.. but those symptoms listed above... that sounds like me. (I don't cry though.. heh.. well, I did when my dog died in june.. but not much.)<P>if anyone read my recent rant about my job, that's just the tip of the iceberg.. my whole life is that messed up.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Go talk to someone. Your GP is a start, as he/she can refer you to a decent councillor or practicing psych. If he wants to try and tackle it himself, he might give you something to help you relax, or to help you sleep. <P>Here's some "official Advice" that I learned while working in Anxiety Research at SBGH: <P><B><I> "If it is in any way interfering with your ability to perform day to day tasks, then it can be considered a problem.</I></B><P>If it DOES interfere with your ability to function, seek help. That goes for BOTH of you.<P>Don't be afraid of Psychologists. You aren't gonna be labelled "insane." Mostly, they will sit there and listen to your problems (which helps a lot), and suggest ways to deal with them. You won't be instituitionalized. Ignore all the movies about the entire Mental Health field. It's TOTALLY wrong. You go see a "shrink," all he'll do is make suggestions. If medication is looking to provide an answer, you'll either be refered to a Psychiatrist, or back to your GP, with a letter of recommendation... Most meds are TEMPORARY measures, to help you get back on track. My prescription was for 3 days... And I didn't finish it.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000011||BC-GT||12-16-2001||05:42 PM||||<B>SBGH</B>&lt;-- cool, thats where I was born..<BR>As far as being depressed, I think most of us here have had some of those symptoms to a certain degree at one point or another, I know I have. If you are a *talker*, find someone whos a good listner, If you are a good *listner*(like myself) just keep yourself occupied by something(anything,really) The thing about getting regular sleep and not oversleeping is really good advice as well.<BR>__good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid27/pe8dae45bb8bf084e93628ae50c338631/fe1839db.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||12-16-2001||05:42 PM||||one morning when I woke up, I asked myself if I had any reason to get up, other than to go to work.. and the answer was no.<P>and if I didn't need the money, I wouldn't be working there.. in fact I don't really want to work anywhere, I feel like the guy from Office Space. I wish I could just go on vacation for a few months to calm down and sort everything out..<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||12-16-2001||05:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>one morning when I woke up, I asked myself if I had any reason to get up, other than to go to work.. and the answer was no.<P>and if I didn't need the money, I wouldn't be working there.. in fact I don't really want to work anywhere, I feel like the guy from Office Space. I wish I could just go on vacation for a few months to calm down and sort everything out..<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Sounds like what you need, man. Go to your doctor, let him know what's going on, and get a week's stress leave. It's perfectly legal, and they can't fire you. Just remember, though, that they CAN start making things difficult for you. Be careful. But remember, you have to put YOURSELF first. Not much point making money if you have to spend it on vending machines within a hospital 'cos you snapped and brought an inflatable squeeky-hammer to work, and laid waste to the entire department...<P>Just go see somebody. Worst-case scenario is that they recommend that you take some stress-leave. Best case is that you just need to get it all out.<P>Again, take care of yourself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||12
Z||000014||Formula||12-16-2001||08:15 PM||||This sounds like one of my friends but she never cries in front of me.  and she says she cries but I'll try to explain it:<P><BR>1) extreme lethargy. Can't get out of bed.<BR>- She sleeps until 2 or 3 in the morning, but she stays out until 4.  But when she doesnt stay out she still sleeps all day.<P>2) Crying fits. Everything overwhelms you to tears on a constant basis.<BR>- She says that shes going to go cry all the time.  She never does it in front of me but I bet she does at home.  But when I try to cheer her up (buying flowers, stuffed animals, taking her out for dinner, etc...) she says shes going to cry, does it matter if shes just emotional?<P>3) Compulsive worrying. Can't get your problems out of your head.<BR>- she worrries about school constantly, she worries about getting Cs on tests (this is college btw) and I think shes really smart so she shouldnt worry about it o much.<P>5) Inability to sleep. Toss-n-turnin', compulsive worrying...<BR>-actually she sleeps like a rock so no problem here.<P>-Should I talk to her about it, or just let it go, I really care for her and don't want her to do something stupid.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||12-16-2001||08:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>This sounds like one of my friends but she never cries in front of me.  and she says she cries but I'll try to explain it:<P><BR>1) extreme lethargy. Can't get out of bed.<BR>- She sleeps until 2 or 3 in the morning, but she stays out until 4.  But when she doesnt stay out she still sleeps all day.<P>2) Crying fits. Everything overwhelms you to tears on a constant basis.<BR>- She says that shes going to go cry all the time.  She never does it in front of me but I bet she does at home.  But when I try to cheer her up (buying flowers, stuffed animals, taking her out for dinner, etc...) she says shes going to cry, does it matter if shes just emotional?<P>3) Compulsive worrying. Can't get your problems out of your head.<BR>- she worrries about school constantly, she worries about getting Cs on tests (this is college btw) and I think shes really smart so she shouldnt worry about it o much.<P>5) Inability to sleep. Toss-n-turnin', compulsive worrying...<BR>-actually she sleeps like a rock so no problem here.<P>-Should I talk to her about it, or just let it go, I really care for her and don't want her to do something stupid.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'm not a shrink!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> all I have is some university pysche courses, and some work experience in a clinic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BUT, again, the determining factor is wether or not her "episodes" are INTERFERING with what she is trying to do? Is she so lethargic that she cannot go to work? Does she cry so much that all the ink runs off her term-paper when she tries to write? <P>Shitty grades might be a suggestion to look into it... But again, the question must be asked "IS IT INTERFERING WITH MY LIFE?" <P>||||reg||6
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||12-17-2001||02:09 PM||||I saw a list a while back of the most stressful things that we go through in life - I think the top three were<P> death of a loved one<BR> Moving far away<BR>  loosing a job<P> stress will make you depressed - but its should be temporary and go away eventually.<P> You moved 4 years ago - while it will not be unusual to miss your old friends and your old life from time to time, if you feel sad all the time then you might be suffering from clinical depression.<P> Depression is a serious illness, and it really is a form of mental illness. It doesnt seem that serious because you only 'feel sad'<P> and you tend to think that someone else, or your current situation is what is causing you to feel this way - the expectation is that someday soon things will change and then you wont feel sad anymore.<P>  There are medical solutions to depression and if you think this might apply to you then you should talk to a doc and look into it. If you hear voices when nobody is there then its obvious that you have a mental illness.<P> But if you feel sad when there is 'nothing there' to make you feel that way - its a very similar situation - your feelings are not a function of what is going on in your life - and thats not good.<P> Imagine what it would be like if someone could do this to you - if someone could put a pill in your orange juice everymorning that would make you feel sad all day long for no apparent reason. If you knew that was happening wouldnt you stop drinkning your orange juice?!<P> well its the same thing, but the other way around - if you are clinically depressed there is medication and other things you can do everyday that will stop you from feeling sad all the time.<P> Depression can also be damaging to your self image and to your relationships with other people - you might tend to think the reason you are depressed is because you are a boring person, or you might blame your depression on someone close to you and end up destroying that relationship.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||12-17-2001||02:13 PM||||also - the fact that you think you might be depressed shows that you are already too far gone!<P>  no wait, or is it the other way around?! :C)<P>BTW the list in the second post in this thread is for major depression - its possible to suffer from less severe forms of depression that still have a major impact on your life. Its best if you get a handle on depression before it gets that severe.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000018||Formula||12-17-2001||03:26 PM||||oh yeah, in my case, her grandpa had a stroke and her aunt and uncle which she was closed to were in car wrecks, then another aunt fell and broke her hip so she has basically been living in the hospital.  I would be depressed from everything she goes through too.||||reg||1
